
JAVA ARRAYLIST INTERVIEW QUESTIONS — MUTABLE, IMMUTABLE & FIXED-SIZE LISTS

S.N. 1
Question:
List<Integer> list = Arrays.asList(10, 20, 30);
list.set(1, 100);
System.out.println(list);

Answer:
[10, 100, 30]

Reason:
Arrays.asList() creates a fixed-size list that allows replacing existing elements.
set(1, 100) replaces 20 with 100. The list size remains unchanged.


S.N. 2
Question:
List<Integer> list = Arrays.asList(10, 20, 30);
list.add(40);

Answer:
UnsupportedOperationException

Reason:
Arrays.asList() creates a fixed-size list. add() attempts to increase the
list size, which is not supported.


S.N. 3
Question:
List<Integer> list = Arrays.asList(10, 20, 30);
list.remove(1);

Answer:
UnsupportedOperationException

Reason:
remove(1) attempts to remove the element at index 1. Arrays.asList()
does not support changing the list size.


S.N. 4
Question:
List<Integer> list = List.of(10, 20, 30);
list.set(1, 100);

Answer:
UnsupportedOperationException

Reason:
List.of() creates an unmodifiable list. You cannot replace, add, or remove
elements through this list.


S.N. 5
Question:
List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(10, 20, 30));
list.set(0, 99);
System.out.println(list);

Answer:
[99, 20, 30]

Reason:
new ArrayList<>(...) creates a separate, mutable copy of the source list.
Therefore, set() works.


S.N. 6
Question:
List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(10, 20, 30));
list.remove(Integer.valueOf(10));
System.out.println(list);

Answer:
[20, 30]

Reason:
Integer.valueOf(10) passes an Integer object to remove(Object), so Java
removes the element with value 10 instead of using 10 as an index.

Important:
remove(1) removes by index.
remove(Integer.valueOf(10)) removes by value.


S.N. 7
Question:
List<Integer> list = Arrays.asList(10, 20, 30);
list.add(1, 15);

Answer:
UnsupportedOperationException

Reason:
add(index, element) attempts to insert an element and increase the list size.
Arrays.asList() does not support this operation.


S.N. 8
Question:
List<Integer> list = Arrays.asList(10, 20, 30);
list.replaceAll(n -> n * 2);
System.out.println(list);

Answer:
[20, 40, 60]

Reason:
replaceAll() replaces existing elements using the supplied operation.
Arrays.asList() supports replacing existing elements.


S.N. 9
Question:
List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(10, 20, 30));
List<Integer> other = list;
other.set(0, 99);
System.out.println(list);

Answer:
[99, 20, 30]

Reason:
Both list and other refer to the same ArrayList object in memory.
Updating the object through other is visible through list.


S.N. 10
Question:
List<Integer> original = new ArrayList<>(List.of(10, 20));
List<Integer> copy = new ArrayList<>(original);
copy.add(30);
System.out.println(original);

Answer:
[10, 20]

Reason:
The ArrayList copy constructor creates a separate list containing the original
elements. Adding 30 to copy does not change original.


S.N. 11
Question:
List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(10, 20, 30));
list.clear();
System.out.println(list);

Answer:
[]

Reason:
A normal ArrayList is mutable and supports clear(), which removes all elements.


S.N. 12
Question:
List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(10, 20));
list.add(null);
System.out.println(list);

Answer:
[10, 20, null]

Reason:
ArrayList allows null elements. Since the list is mutable, add(null)
successfully appends null to the list.


QUICK REVISION

List Type                  Replace       Add/Remove       Allows null
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
new ArrayList<>()           Yes           Yes              Yes
Arrays.asList(...)          Yes           No               Yes
List.of(...)                No            No               No

INTERVIEW CONCLUSION

1. Mutable:
   The list's elements or size can be changed.

2. Fixed-size:
   Existing elements can be replaced, but elements cannot be added or removed.
   Example: Arrays.asList(...)

3. Unmodifiable:
   Modifications through that list reference are not allowed.
   Example: List.of(...)

4. Mutable copy:
   new ArrayList<>(source) creates a separate, resizable list containing
   the source elements.
